extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Features | Annotations"
	- pagedescription = "Annotations in Tablechart: brackets, trend arrows and reference lines, placed by the data."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Annotations
				p.lead A bracket, a trend arrow or a reference line, placed by the data.

	+section("How to add an annotation")
		p Put a #[code div] with the class #[code chart-annotations] inside the chart. Each annotation in it is a #[code span] with #[code data-annotation]. An annotation is placed by the data, so if you change a number, the annotation moves with it.

		+codeblock('HTML')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table …
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket") +11%

	+section("Bracket")
		p A bracket shows the change between two bars. If the chart has two bars, no other attributes are needed.

		+chart(null, '9')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apple juice
					br
					span thousands of litres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Last season', '251.5'], ['This season', '279.6']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket") +11%

	+section("Choose the bars")
		p If the chart has more bars, set #[code data-from] and #[code data-to] to the row numbers. The first row is #[code 1].

		+chart(null, '11')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2021', '74'], ['2022', '80'], ['2023', '91'], ['2024', '102'], ['2025', '116']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="bracket" data-from="2" data-to="5") +45%

	+section("Trend")
		p A trend arrow runs from the first value to the last. #[code data-at] sets where the badge is along the arrow, from #[code 0] to #[code 1].
		p Set #[code data-years] to the number of years. Tablechart calculates the yearly rate, and shows a warning in the console if it is different from yours. Your number is always the one shown.
		p A #[code span] with the class #[code tablechart-caption] is shown above the badge.

		+chart(null, '12')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apples per tree
					br
					span each
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2021', '74'], ['2022', '80'], ['2023', '91'], ['2024', '102'], ['2025', '116']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="trend" data-years="4" data-at="0.46")
						span.tablechart-caption A year
						span +12%

	+section("Reference line")
		p A reference line marks a value, such as a target or the mean. To put it on the value axis, add #[code data-value]. The line then goes across a column, line or area chart, and up a bar chart. #[code data-value] is a number, #[code mean] or #[code median]. The mean and the median are of the values in the table.

		+chart(null, '11')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption: b Apples picked
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Spring', '58'], ['Summer', '72'], ['Autumn', '70'], ['Winter', '41']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="reference" data-value="60") Target

		p If the names of the rows are numbers, add #[code data-name] to put the line among them, as if the names were a scale. Here each row is a number of apples on a branch, and its value is how many branches have that many. If you use #[code mean] or #[code median], each name counts as often as its value: the mean is the mean number of apples on a branch.

		+chart(null, '13')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption: b Branches by number of apples
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['2', '3'], ['3', '8'], ['4', '12'], ['5', '9'], ['6', '5'], ['7', '2']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="reference" data-name="mean") Mean 4.3
					span(data-annotation="reference" data-name="median" data-line="dotted") Median 4

		p On a scatter chart, #[code data-value] is a value on the axis across, so the line goes up. To put the line across, at a value on the axis up, add #[code data-axis="y"]. The mean and the median are then of the column of that axis.

		+chart(null, '13')
			figure(data-chart="scatter")
				figcaption: b Harvest and price per variety
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Harvest per tree (kg)
							th Price per kg (€)
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '62', '1.20'], ['Jonagold', '75', '1.05'], ['Braeburn', '48', '1.45'], ['Gala', '55', '1.30'], ['Cox', '35', '1.85'], ['Discovery', '30', '1.75']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="reference" data-value="1.5" data-axis="y") €1.50

		p The line is dashed. If you add #[code data-line="dotted"], it is dotted. The text of the annotation is the label, on the line. If two labels would cover each other, the second one moves beside its line, or one line lower. If the annotation has no text, there is only the line. Where the line crosses a number or a name on the chart, it stops a little before it and goes on after it.
		p If the label has a number and the line uses #[code mean] or #[code median], Tablechart checks it, to the decimals you wrote, and shows a warning in the console if they differ. Your text is always the one shown.

	+section("Quadrant")
		p A quadrant divides a scatter chart in four, with a line up and a line across, and names each part in its outer corner. The lines are not in the middle unless you want them there: #[code data-x] and #[code data-y] say where they are, as a number, #[code mean] or #[code median]. Each name has #[code data-corner]: #[code top left], #[code top right], #[code bottom left] or #[code bottom right]. The quadrant comes with the scatter chart type, so it works once you add #[code scatter].

		+chart(null, '20')
			figure(data-chart="scatter" data-chart-grid)
				figcaption: b Varieties at the market
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Variety
							th Share of sales (%)
							th Growth (%)
					tbody
						each row in [['Elstar', '24', '1.2'], ['Jonagold', '18', '-0.8'], ['Braeburn', '12', '2.9'], ['Gala', '9', '4.1'], ['Kanzi', '6', '6.3'], ['Granny Smith', '4', '-1.5'], ['Wellant', '3', '3.4'], ['Santana', '2', '0.4'], ['Cox', '7', '-2.2'], ['Boskoop', '11', '-0.3']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
				.chart-annotations
					span(data-annotation="quadrant" data-x="10" data-y="1")
						span(data-corner="top left") Rising
						span(data-corner="top right") Stars
						span(data-corner="bottom left") Fading
						span(data-corner="bottom right") Steady

		p The quadrant is there from the start, before the dots. Its lines are whole until the name of a dot arrives over them; then the line makes room for the name. A dot name that would cover a corner name is left out, and the tooltip of its dot still has it. Screen readers read the names of the corners with the annotations, under the table.
		p With #[code data-fragment] on the figure, the axes and the quadrant are shown first, and all dots arrive on the next step. Then you can explain the quadrants before the data comes in. See #[a(href=url('/features/steps.html')) Step by step].
		p The lines are dashed by default. To change them, set the #[code --tablechart-quadrant-] variables: see #[a(href=url('/reference/variables.html#annotations')) CSS variables].

	+section("Notes")
		p A note says what happened at a row, such as a “Share issue” or a “New apple press”. A note is not an annotation you write under the table, but a column in the table: a column whose header has #[code data-note]. Each cell with text is a note at its row. The chart has a line at that row, with the note at the top.

		+chart(null, '5,10')
			figure(data-chart="column")
				figcaption
					b Apple juice sold
					br
					span thousands of litres
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Quarter
							th Litres
							th(data-note) Note
					tbody
						each row in [['Q1 2024', '58', ''], ['Q2 2024', '61', ''], ['Q3 2024', '84', 'New apple press'], ['Q4 2024', '90', ''], ['Q1 2025', '88', '']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p The note is in the table, so a screen reader reads it with its row, a printed table has it, and a translation script translates it like any other cell. If you point at the row, or tap it, the tooltip shows the note too. A row without a note has an empty cell.
		p A note works on a chart with its rows across: a column, line, area or waterfall chart. In a summary, #[code data-each="notes"] lists them: see #[a(href=url('/features/summary.html#notes')) Summary]. From a JSON file, a row has its note in #[code note]: see #[a(href=url('/data.html#json-structure')) Data].

	+section("Break the scale")
		p A break in the scale is not an annotation, but it is added the same way. See #[a(href=url('/features/scale-break.html')) Scale break].

	+section("Annotations on several lines")
		p On a chart with several lines, #[code data-series] sets which line an annotation uses. The first line is #[code 1], and is used if #[code data-series] is not set.

		+codeblock('HTML')
			span(data-annotation="trend" data-series="2" data-years="9") +8%
